Voting in France has traditionally not been cutting-edge technology: the voters close in the boothroom and place a pre-printed piece of paper on the envelope, on which they have marked the candidate of their choice.
After the authorities have confirmed the voter's identity, the voter drops the envelope into the ballot box and signs the voting list.
France's electoral legislation has stipulated the course of events relatively accurately.
In 1988, voting boxes were ordered to be transparent so that voters and observers can prove that there are no envelopes in the drawer at the start of the vote and that no envelopes other than officially counted and authorised votes were added to the box.
Candidates may send representatives to prove every part of the series of events. In the evening, volunteers count the votes under close supervision and complying with the prescribed procedures.
