When a small group of organisms (a small population) is separated from the main population from which it is native (for example, if it moves across a mountain range or river or to a new island, so that it is not easy to move back), its environment is often different than before.
Resources for a new environment are different and competitors are different, so in order to be a strong rival, the new population needs different characteristics or adaptations than before.
The original population has not changed at all, but still needs the same adjustments as before.
When the new population begins to adapt to its new environment, it begins to look less and less like the main population over time.
In the end, after thousands or even millions of years, both populations look so different from each other that they can no longer be called the same species.
We call this procession specification, which only means the emergence of a new species. Specification is the necessary consequence of evolution and a very important part of it.
