Technological determinism is a term that, in practice, covers a wide range of ideas about the thrust of so-called technology, i.e. the necessity of technology, to such an absolute notion that human fate is guided by logic based on scientific laws and their manifestation in technology.
In most interpretations of technological determinism, there are two common basic ideas: firstly, that the development of technology itself follows a route that is largely beyond cultural and political influence, and, secondly, that technology, in turn, has intrinsic rather than socially conditioned ‘effects’ on societies.
As an example, the discovery of a car inevitably leads to the development of roads.
On the other hand, the nationwide road network is not economically viable for just a few cars, so new production methods are being developed to reduce the cost of owning a car.
The mass ownership of cars results in several accidents on the road, which leads to the discovery of new health care methods to repair damaged bodies.
Cultural determinism was a significant element in romanticism and drawn from writers such as Goethe, Fichte and Schlegel.
In the context of romance, geography was considered to shape individuals. Over time, the customs and culture typical of this geographical location were formed. Because these were in harmony with society, they functioned better than arbitrarily enacted laws.
Constantinople was considered the fashion capital of the feudal Europe in the same way that Paris is known as the fashion capital of today's world.
Its reputation at the center of luxury began around the year of A.D. 400 and continued until about 1100.
Its position deteriorated in the 11th century, mainly because the Crusaders had returned home with gifts such as silk and spices, which were appreciated more than what the Byzantine market has to offer.
At that very time, the title of the fashion capital was transferred from Constantinople to Paris.
The Gothic style peaked between the turn of the 900's and 1000's and the 13th century.
Initially, the clothes were heavily influenced by the Byzantine culture of the East.
