The stirrups are the rider's foot restraints that hang on either side of the saddle.
Riders get more stability from them, but they may be accompanied by safety concerns, as the rider's legs may cling to them.
If the rider falls off the horse's back, but the leg gets caught in the stirrup, the escaping horse may pull him behind him. Various precautions may be taken to reduce this risk.
First of all, most riders wear heeled riding boots with a smooth and quite narrow sole.
Some saddles, especially English, have a safety rod, thanks to which the stirrup strap detaches from the saddle, if the falling rider pulls it back.
